WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:05.639
At the beginning of this pandemic like everyone
I was hearing lots about viruses, but realised

00:00:05.639 --> 00:00:08.639
I didn’t know that much about what they
are.

00:00:08.639 --> 00:00:12.760
So I did a load of research and have summarised
what I learned in these nine images.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.630
Okay let’s get into it.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.740
Viruses are the most abundant form of life
on Earth.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:19.500
But, are they life?

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:20.500
Not really.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.140
They can’t reproduce on their own so they
have to invade other cells to take over their

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.899
molecular machinery to reproduce using the
steps shown here which I’ll go into more

00:00:28.899 --> 00:00:30.230
detail later.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:35.390
There’s an unknown number of different kinds
of virus, definitely in the millions, if not

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:36.390
higher.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.920
But only about six thousand have been studied
in detail.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:44.110
They vary wildly, invading all kinds of cells,
animals, plants, bacteria and archaea and

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:47.840
none of them work in precisely the same way.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.609
Despite this bewildering range it is possible
to make some categorisations, although please

00:00:52.609 --> 00:00:55.980
be aware that there are exceptions to any
category that I’m defining.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.640
That’s the nature of any subject as complex
as virology.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:05.690
With that in mind viruses always contain a
genome, the instructions for making more viruses,

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:10.580
and a protective shell made of proteins called
a capsid which keeps the virus safe and helps

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:13.880
the virus stick to and enter the cells they’re
invading.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.500
Some viruses are also coated in an envelope,
a greasy cover taken from the membrane of

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.460
the last cell they infected.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:23.630
Viruses are incredibly small.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:27.880
For example an average human cell is a bit
smaller than a tenth of a millimetre or a

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.900
hundred micrometres.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:35.780
And viruses are around a thousand times smaller
than that, ranging from about 20 nanometres

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:36.780
upwards.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.590
Here are a few virus particles drawn to scale.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:46.710
Hepatitis A, hepatitis C, coronavirus, influenza,
hiv and the humongous ebolavirus.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:53.300
For some viruses their genome is really small
coding for just two proteins, but other viruses

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:58.710
code for up to 2500 proteins for the largest
genomes.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.170
There are a few ways to classify viruses.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:05.550
The genome of a virus is either based on DNA
or RNA.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.750
And is either single stranded or double stranded.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.580
Viruses come in a wide range of shapes.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:14.820
The capsid of viruses are made of many of
the same shaped proteins that self assemble

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:19.910
into different shapes like helical which is
a spiral, icosahedral, made of shapes like

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:24.450
triangles or hexagons or pentagon to make
a shape like a twenty sided dice or soccer

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:25.600
ball.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.130
Prolate which is the same kind of thing but
stretched out.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:34.140
Complex which covers a wide range of viruses
that don’t fit into the other categories,

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:37.420
and finally enveloped viruses which we mentioned
earlier.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:43.080
There is a scientific way to classify viruses
by following the official taxonomy.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.440
Another useful classification technique is
the baltimore classification, which looks

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:52.410
at the genome of a virus, and it’s pathway
to encoding messenger RNA or mRNA which is

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:57.730
a single stranded molecule that the host cell
uses to make the virus proteins.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:03.069
There are seven classes in the baltimore classification
which represent different forms of single

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:06.030
and double stranded DNA and RNA.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:09.569
For an example et’s look at the coronavirus
that caused the 2020 pandemic.

00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:15.100
It is an enveloped virus, meaning that it
is very vulnerable to soap which rips of the

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.680
outer protective coat destroying it, which
is why we’ve been told to wash our hands

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.319
so often.

00:03:21.319 --> 00:03:25.150
And in the baltimore classification it is
a class four virus which is a type of single

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:27.880
stranded RNA virus.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:32.880
Interestingly this is different to seasonal
flu which is a class five virus, so the two

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.020
are not related.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:40.110
Now let’s look in a bit more detail about
how viruses get into cells, how they reproduce

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:42.610
and how they get back out again.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:46.840
There are three main ways viruses get their
genetic material into a cell.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:50.610
Enveloped viruses attach to receptors on the
surface of the cell which then fuses with

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:51.910
the membrane.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:55.709
Remember they are made of the same stuff because
the virus took it’s envelope from the last

00:03:55.709 --> 00:04:00.600
cell it was in, so the new cell doesn’t
see that it is an invader.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:04.130
Viruses without an envelope essentially trick
the cell into thinking that the virus is a

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:07.580
harmless resource like nutrition, and engulfs
it.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:13.459
This is called endocytosis and viruses with
an envelope can enter a cell this way too.

00:04:13.459 --> 00:04:17.480
This virus then has to break out of this vesicle
to release its genetic material.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:22.539
A less common method of entering a cell is
genetic injection where the virus punches

00:04:22.539 --> 00:04:28.569
a hole in the cell membrane and directly injects
its genetic material into the cell.

00:04:28.569 --> 00:04:33.360
Bacteriophages do this to bacteria, but not
to human cells.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.060
Replication is a very complex process so I’m
having to break it down into perhaps an overly

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.680
simplistic description, but at least it’s
a start.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.969
In general RNA viruses replicate in the cell
cytoplasm, and DNA viruses replicate in the

00:04:46.969 --> 00:04:50.500
cell’s nucleus, but there are exceptions
to this.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.520
The host cell then starts transcribing the
viral genetic material without knowing that

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:56.860
it is not native to the cell.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:02.069
The virus codes for essentially two things,
an enzyme called polymerase and proteins which

00:05:02.069 --> 00:05:04.800
form the capsid shell.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.529
Polymerase creates copies of the viral genome.

00:05:07.529 --> 00:05:10.690
This is also when mutations can happen to
the virus.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:16.409
In general viruses mutate very fast, RNA viruses
more quickly than DNA viruses because RNA

00:05:16.409 --> 00:05:19.650
is inherently less stable than DNA.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:23.680
These mutations are random, most having little
effect on the virus, but sometimes they can

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:27.250
make the virus more dangerous or less dangerous
to the host.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:32.030
The capsid proteins self assemble, from a
couple of simple shapes they can form large

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:37.370
complicated three dimensional structures which
enclose copies of the genetic material inside,

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:40.650
to form a new virus particle ready to leave
the cell.

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:45.400
It’s worth mentioning retroviruses which
are a type of virus that insert their viral

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:48.249
DNA into the DNA of their host.

00:05:48.249 --> 00:05:52.719
This has happened a lot through our evolutionary
history and in fact viral DNA sequences make

00:05:52.719 --> 00:05:58.779
up 8% of our genome, a lot of which we share
with our common ancestors.

00:05:58.779 --> 00:06:01.180
There are three ways viruses leave cells.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:06.139
Apoptosis results in the death of the cell
through a self-destruct mechanism.

00:06:06.139 --> 00:06:11.330
The viruses either burst out as shown here,
or more usually the cell death is a more controlled

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:17.389
process where sections clump off to be absorbed
by other cells like macrophages.

00:06:17.389 --> 00:06:21.830
Budding is where the virus exits the cell,
taking with it an envelope of the cell’s

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:22.830
membrane.

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:26.610
This doesn’t kill the cell, but will degrades
it over time and will eventually lead to the

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:27.719
cell’s death.

00:06:27.719 --> 00:06:33.270
A kind of opposite process is exocytosis where
the virus has an envelope inside the cell,

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:37.439
which it got from the nucleus membrane, or
another membrane in the cell.

00:06:37.439 --> 00:06:42.069
Then the virus exits through the cell wall
leaving the membrane behind.

00:06:42.069 --> 00:06:43.559
And this doesn’t kill the cell.

00:06:43.559 --> 00:06:49.279
I should also mention that some viruses can
stay dormant inside cells for years at a time

00:06:49.279 --> 00:06:51.960
which is called latency.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:56.139
So that’s how viruses invade cells, now
lets zoom out and look at a broader picture

00:06:56.139 --> 00:06:59.169
of viral infections.

00:06:59.169 --> 00:07:04.169
The range of a virus is how many different
kinds of organism the virus can infect.

00:07:04.169 --> 00:07:08.649
Plant viruses don't infect animals and most
animal viruses can't infect humans.

00:07:08.649 --> 00:07:13.849
In most cases viruses are adapted to a single
species, but some like rabies have a larger

00:07:13.849 --> 00:07:17.749
range and others can cross over to other species
when they mutate.

00:07:17.749 --> 00:07:22.419
If we look at humans we can group viruses
into those that have been with us for a long

00:07:22.419 --> 00:07:25.889
time, and those which have recently crossed
over.

00:07:25.889 --> 00:07:30.660
Equilibrium viruses have had a long time to
adapt to our biology so tend not to be lethal

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:31.660
to us.

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:36.009
After all it is more effective for the virus
to keep us alive to infect other people rather

00:07:36.009 --> 00:07:37.949
than killing us.

00:07:37.949 --> 00:07:42.559
Non-equlibrium viruses are a lot more dangerous
because the virus is not adapted to our biology

00:07:42.559 --> 00:07:47.689
and so the mortality rate from these infections
is a lot higher as different people’s immune

00:07:47.689 --> 00:07:49.960
systems react to them in many different ways.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:54.689
Here are some examples of non-equilibrium
viruses including of course the coronavirus

00:07:54.689 --> 00:07:58.099
pandemic we are currently experiencing.

00:07:58.099 --> 00:08:03.369
Influenza has got a segmented genome, a genome
in many parts which means that different strains

00:08:03.369 --> 00:08:06.899
can swap genes if they infect the same host.

00:08:06.899 --> 00:08:11.469
This means they are more likely to cross over
species and is called antigenic shift, which

00:08:11.469 --> 00:08:15.319
is what happened in the 2009 H1N1 epidemic.

00:08:15.319 --> 00:08:22.120
Our bodies have got sophisticated mechanisms
to detect and destroy viral infections.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:27.569
It starts when a virus is ingested by an antigen-presenting
cell which breaks it down and displays portions

00:08:27.569 --> 00:08:31.210
of the virus to activate T-helper cells.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:35.029
The T-helper cells then activate two responses
to the virus.

00:08:35.029 --> 00:08:39.519
B cells produce antibodies that are targeted
at the specific virus.

00:08:39.519 --> 00:08:44.039
These antibodies bind to the outer surface
of the virus neutralising it, they also tag

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:49.089
the virus to be destroyed if it enters a cell
by enzymes within the cell.

00:08:49.089 --> 00:08:54.340
Cells in our body continually display what
proteins they have inside them on their surface.

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:59.540
If a cytotoxic T cell recognises a viral protein
on the surface of an infected cell, it will

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:01.260
go and destroy that cell.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:05.870
Our body’s B and T cell’s keep a memory
of the virus which can make us immune to future

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:11.020
infections from that specific virus, but this
immunity wears off over time.

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:14.740
Sometimes this immunity lasts many years,
but other times like for the viruses that

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:19.120
cause the common cold and flu, our immunity
is not very good because of the high variability

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:23.589
in these viruses, or because our antibody
response is poor.

00:09:23.589 --> 00:09:26.579
Vaccines are incredibly effective ways of
preventing viral infections.

00:09:26.579 --> 00:09:31.950
It is possible to completely eradicate viral
infections of human equilibrium viruses which

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:37.480
we’ve managed with smallpox, and is possible
with polio, measles, mumps and rubella if

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:39.690
only people would listen.

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:44.500
Vaccines contain a modified form of the virus
that has been weakened in some way so that

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:49.410
they no longer cause an infection but they
do stimulate an immune response, teaching

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:52.620
our immune system how to recognise the virus.

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:58.339
And where there’s no vaccine antiviral drugs
can be used to treat viral infections.

00:09:58.339 --> 00:10:02.600
Although development of these drugs is difficult
because they only target a specific virus

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.449
which are continually mutating.

00:10:05.449 --> 00:10:09.800
One example of an antiviral technique are
drugs that fool the virus into incorporating

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:14.459
dummy DNA into their genomes which stops them
from having the instructions to replicate

00:10:14.459 --> 00:10:16.339
any more.

00:10:16.339 --> 00:10:20.801
So that covers everything I wanted to talk
about, if you want to find out more there

00:10:20.801 --> 00:10:23.110
are a bunch of links in the video description.

00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:27.649
You’ll also find a link to this image which
I’d love for you to share around the internet,

00:10:27.649 --> 00:10:32.620
as well as this video, I think the more people
who get access to good information about viruses

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:34.810
right now the better.

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:38.990
This content wouldn’t be possible without
the support of my patrons and people buying

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:39.990
posters.

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:42.010
So if you’d like help out please consider
it.

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:45.810
I hope you are all doing okay and I’ll see
you with more science soon.

